sec.p:
The study leveraged two public datasets to conduct both internal and external validation, ensuring the model’s performance generalizes effectively. For model training and validation, we used data from the I-SPY 2 trial (Investigation of Serial studies to Predict Your Therapeutic Response with Imaging And Molecular Analysis 2), a multi-center clinical trial designed to evaluate the efficacy of experimental drugs in breast cancer treatment in the NAC setting [15, 16]. The I-SPY 2 cohort included 985 patients, of which 660 were curated for model training and validation. For external validation, we used the I-SPY 1 dataset, consisting of 114 curated patients, allowing us to evaluate the model’s generalizability across institutions and imaging protocols [17]. Both datasets are multi-institutional. In addition, the I-SPY 2 MRIs are axial, whereas I-SPY 1 MRIs are sagittal. The patient characteristics (demographics, pCR, and clinical features) for the two datasets are shown in Table 1A, B, respectively.Table 1
Patient characteristics of (A) I-SPY 2 and (B) I-SPY-1 cohort. Values are the count and percentages, except for age which is mean and standard deviation. Treatment types were not modeled
