I-SPY 2 (Investigation of Serial studies to Predict Your Therapeutic Response with Imaging and moLecular analysis 2/ACRIN 6698) is an ongoing, multi-center clinical trial designed to evaluate the efficacy of novel agents in breast cancer treatment in the neoadjuvant chemotherapy setting. A total of 719 patients were included in the first collection, but in combination with 266 patients from the ACRIN 6698/I-SPY2 Breast DWI Collection, a total of 985 patients comprises the entire cohort (Fig. 1A). Women with locally advanced breast cancer (stage II or III, tumor \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\ge 2.5$$\end{document}≥2.5 cm) are adaptively randomized to receive standard NAC or one of several experimental therapies based on early imaging responses. The study utilizes dynamic contrast-enhanced MRI (DCE-MRI) at multiple time points to monitor tumor response during NAC. Each patient had clinical non-imaging data of which included the drug arm treatment category, hormone receptor (HR) status, human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2) status, age, race, menopausal status, and ethnicity. The only available outcome variable is PCR, which is 1 for pathological complete response. For prediction, only the hormone receptor status, human epidermal growth factor 2 status, age, race, and menopausal status were used. While the I-SPY 2 dataset includes multiple treatment arms, treatment type was excluded from modeling to focus on features available across both datasets.Fig. 1
A Exclusion/inclusion flowchart for I-SPY 2. B Exclusion/inclusion flowchart for I-SPY 1

