Class imbalance posed a challenge in predicting pCR outcomes, with non-pCR cases being more prevalent (68%). To address this, we used a combination of binary cross-entropy (BCE) and focal loss, defined as below. BCE was included to maintain stable gradient flow for well-classified samples, while focal loss was added to down-weight the majority class samples and prioritize the harder-to-classify positive cases [24, 25]. We empirically found that scaling each loss equally by a factor of 0.5 provided the highest AUC performance.\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{c}{\text{L}}{\text{o}}{\text{s}}{\text{s}}=0.5*{L}_{BCE}+0.5*{L}_{focal}\\ {L}_{BCE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}-log({p}_{t})\\ {L}_{focal}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}-{\alpha }_{t}(1-{p}_{t}{)}^{\gamma }log({p}_{t})\end{array}$$\end{document}Loss=0.5∗LBCE+0.5∗LfocalLBCE=1N∑i=1N-log(pt)Lfocal=1N∑i=1N-αt(1-pt)γlog(pt)

We compared the performance of our attention-fused multimodal deep learning model against four traditional machine learning (ML) methods: Support Vector Machine (SVM) with the RBF Kernel, logistic regression, random forest, and XGBoost. These methods were provided with features extracted from our trained encoder of our deep learning model (Fig. 6). Specifically, we extracted the encoded features for the three input streams (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
