Table 3A and B summarize the performance of our deep learning model against traditional ML methods on the two datasets. On the I-SPY 2 dataset, our deep learning model outperformed all four traditional ML methods in both AUC and accuracy, indicating its superior ability to distinguish between positive and negative cases. On the I-SPY 1 dataset, our model achieved a slightly higher AUC compared to the traditional methods but achieves similar levels with accuracy. Notably, the sensitivity of the traditional ML methods is consistently lower across both datasets, suggesting that these models are more prone to biasing their predictions towards the majority class (pCR negative). In contrast, our model exhibits a more balanced performance, as evidenced by its higher sensitivity, which is critical for detecting positive cases in this clinical context. The relatively strong performance of the traditional ML methods, which use the trained deep learning model’s encoder’s output as input features, emphasizes the strength of our model’s encoder in capturing meaningful and discriminative latent representations. However, the fact that the traditional methods using the same encoded features still underperformed compared to our attention-based model suggests that the multimodal fusion part of the model is also crucial for predicting pCR from imaging and non-imaging data.Table 3
